# UiPilot + target app workflow

UiPilot is under active development. When using it to drive a target desktop app (WPF via
`UiPilot.Wpf`, Avalonia via `UiPilot.Avalonia`):

## Extend the shared stack when needed

If the target app exposes a gap (menu navigation, window state, screenshots while minimized,
elevated lifecycle, multi-app sessions, etc.), **add the feature to UiPilot.Core / the matching
adapter / Cli** rather than working around it in one-off scripts.

- Shared protocol/tools belong in `UiPilot.Core`; WPF-only behavior in `UiPilot.Wpf`; Avalonia-only in `UiPilot.Avalonia`; multi-app lifecycle in `UiPilot.Cli`.
- Keep changes minimal and reusable for any app on that UI stack.
- **Never add product-specific selectors, paths, credentials, workflows, scenarios, or tests to UiPilot.**
  Durable product tests belong in the product repository and reference `UiPilot.Client`.
- Add or update unit/integration tests for new behavior.
- Run `dotnet test` and ensure **all existing tests pass** before finishing.
- **Check in** (`git commit`) when pilot code changed — do not leave edits uncommitted.

## Driving the target app

- Launch with `UIPILOT_START_MINIMIZED=1` (CLI edit loop does this) so the agent/IDE stays visible; use `bring_to_front` only when a human must see the window.
- Screenshots via `RenderTargetBitmap` work while minimized — prefer that over keeping the app foregrounded.
- For elevated targets (`requireAdministrator`), run Cursor/CLI elevated so start/stop works without UAC prompts.
- For two UIs: `start_app`/`attach` with distinct `session` names (`server`, `client`), pass
  `session` on tools or `select_session`, and remember element ids are per session. Use `stop_all`
  to tear down.
- Prefer `start_app` / `build_and_start` (process-scoped `DOTNET_STARTUP_HOOKS`) so the target app does not need `PilotHost.Start()` or a UiPilot package reference. In-app `PilotHost.Start()` remains optional and idempotent.
- For non-UI hosts (e.g. a supervisor console): `start_process` + generic `wait_for_log(pathOrGlob, pattern)` — keep product-specific paths/regex in project rules, not in UiPilot.

## Target app changes

UI fixes belong in the **target app repo** (e.g. `EL_UI`), not in UiPilot. UiPilot only gets automation/protocol improvements.
